keep a gba cart from dragging the handheld down

Picking Pokemon Emerald and going back to the shelf took the whole frontend
down to one frame every one to nine seconds, and an earlier GBA launch was
OOM-killed. Three causes, all on the Flip's 128 MB and two A7 cores:

- GBA defaulted to mGBA, which interprets and cannot hold 60 fps on a GBA
  cart here; gpSP has an ARM dynarec and does. The guide said to pick gpSP
  by hand, and a card with no Config/selected_core.txt never had. GBA now
  defaults to gpSP, and the core picker seats on the same default. mGBA is
  still one press away. This changes upstream's default, forced by the CPU.
- 0031 made up missed presents, clamped to three. A load that stalled the
  display for seconds was read as three presents owed, every present, so
  the stall fed itself. More than three blanks is now a stall and is let go,
  on the display side and the core side alike.
- the prefetch read the whole ROM on every pause in the list: 16 MB for
  Emerald, 340 ms of card I/O, and page cache that pushed slot's own code
  out of memory. It now stops at 4 MB; the cores page the rest in.

Applies on top of 0032 (draw the game straight out under a hud), upstream 91499a9.

